cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "LLM consistency on nursing clinical judgement: raw responses, per-cell metrics, and analysis code"
message: >-
  If you use this dataset or code, please cite both the article and this
  repository.
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Tajima
    given-names: Hiroyuki
    affiliation: "Faculty of Nursing, Shumei University, Japan"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3817-4455"
abstract: >-
  Raw API responses, analysis scripts, scenario texts, item-selection code,
  and figure-generation code for a two-study evaluation of the response
  consistency of three commercial large language models (GPT-4o, Claude Opus
  4.5, Gemini 2.5 Flash-Lite) in nursing clinical judgement, framed as a
  deployment-safety evaluation of whether output consistency can serve as a
  pre-deployment safety signal. Study 2, the primary analysis, is an
  independent cross-lingual external validation on 90 publicly released items
  of the Japanese national nursing examination (29,700 API calls); Study 1,
  reported as supplementary, uses 30 author-written English scenarios (9,836
  valid of 9,900 attempted calls). Response consistency is measured as
  1 - H(R) / log2(k), the normalised complement of the Shannon entropy of the
  empirical response distribution (k = number of options), and is reported
  jointly with accuracy.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - response consistency
  - nursing clinical judgement
  - clinical decision support
  - patient safety
  - deployment safety
  - reproducibility
  - cross-lingual validation
